WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Our Earth is the only known planet that sustains life,

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.000
and it does so in abundance.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.000
I have been fortunate enough over the years

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.480
to travel to some of the most extraordinary and remote places on Earth

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.160
to find and film animals.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.720
This is the biggest flower in the world.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.600
The blue whale!

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.320
It's the biggest creature that exists on the planet.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.520
The sheer number and variety of animals and plants is astonishing.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.000
Estimates of the number of different species

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.280
vary from six million to 100 million.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.560
Nobody knows exactly how many different kinds of animals there are here.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.800
Wherever you look, there's life.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.320
There are often a multitude of variations on a single pattern.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.880
Nearly 200 different kinds of monkeys, for example.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.680
And 315 hummingbirds.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:22.680
Nearly 1,000 bats.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.000
And beetles, at least 350,000 species of them.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Not to mention a quarter of a million different kinds of flowering plants.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.080
The variety is astounding.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.160
Even in this one small English woodland,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.160
you might see four or five different kinds of finches.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.520
Why should there be such a dazzling variety?

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.600
And how can we make sense of such a huge range of living organisms?

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.240
200 years ago, a man was born

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.040
who was to explain this astonishing diversity of life.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.360
In doing so, he revolutionised the way in which we see the world

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.720
and our place in it.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:57.480
His name was Charles Darwin.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.040
This book, the Holy Bible,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:05.040
explains how this wonderful diversity came about.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.760
On the third day after the creation of the world,

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.560
God created plants.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.360
On the fifth day, fish and birds.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.760
And then on the sixth day, mammals.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.560
And finally, man.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:26.840
That explanation was believed literally by pretty well

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.840
the whole of Western Europe for the best part of 2,000 years.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.920
And generations of painters pictured it for the faithful.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.640
This version was painted in Italy in the 16th century.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.160
Here is God in the Garden of Eden,

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.960
which is now filled with all kinds of animals.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.040
Here he is pulling Adam out of the earth.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:59.040
And here creating the first woman by putting Adam to sleep

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:05.120
and then taking one of his ribs and extracting Eve from his side.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.520
And she comes out assisted by two angels.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:17.520
And when God had finished, he said to Adam and Eve,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.600
be fruitful and multiply and replenish the earth and subdue it.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:27.600
And have dominion over the fish of the sea and over the fowl of the air

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.680
and over every living thing that moveth upon the earth.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.760
That made it clear that according to the Bible,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.840
humanity could exploit the natural world as they wished.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.920
This view of mankind's superiority still stood when in 1831,

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.920
a British surveying ship, the Beagle,

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.000
set off on a voyage around the world.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.680
On board, as a companion to the captain,

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.760
was the 22-year-old Charles Darwin.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.800
They crossed the Atlantic and made landfall on the coast of Brazil.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.440
There, the sheer abundance of tropical nature

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.320
astonishes the newcomer,

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:21.800
as I discovered when I retraced Darwin's steps 30 years ago

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.880
for a television series about the diversity of nature.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.840
Darwin, as a boy, had been a fanatical collector of insects,

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:35.120
and here he was enthralled almost to the point of ecstasy.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.640
In one day, in a small area,

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:41.720
he discovered 69 different species of beetle.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.760
As he wrote in his journal,

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.760
it's enough to disturb the composure of the entomologist's mind

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.840
to contemplate the future dimension of a complete catalogue.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.880
They went south, rounded Cape Horn, and so reached the Pacific.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:08.480
And then, in September 1835,

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:11.360
after they'd been away for almost four years,

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.360
they landed on the little-known islands of the Galapagos.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.640
Here, they found creatures that existed nowhere else in the world,

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:23.720
cormorants that had lost the power of flight,

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:29.480
lizards that swam out through the surf to graze on the bottom of the sea.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:37.920
Darwin, who had studied botany and geology at Cambridge University,

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.320
collected specimens of the animals and plants,

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.080
and, as usual, when he went ashore to investigate,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.160
described what he found in his journal.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:55.560
My servant and self were landed a few miles to the north-east

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.640
in order that I might examine the district mentioned above

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:06.280
as resembling chimneys, organic chimneys, presumably.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.760
The comparison would have been more exact

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.840
if I'd sent the iron furnaces near Wolverhampton.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.840
The British resident in the Galapagos claimed that he knew

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:27.520
from the shape of a giant tortoise's shell

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.120
which island it had come from.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:34.040
If it had a rounded front, it came from a well-watered island

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.880
where it fed on lush ground plants,

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.280
whereas one from a drier island had a peak at the front,

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:45.560
which enabled it to reach up to higher vegetation.

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:54.440
Were these tortoises, each on their separate islands, different species?

00:07:54.440 --> 00:08:02.920
And if so, was each one a separate act of divine creation?

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:08.280
The differences that Darwin had noticed amongst these Galapagos animals

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.560
were, of course, all tiny.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.040
But if they could develop, wasn't it possible

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.840
that over the thousands or millions of years,

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.000
a whole series of such differences

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:21.240
might add up to one revolutionary challenge?

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:25.920
On his voyage home, Darwin had time to ponder on these things.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.200
Could it be that species were not fixed for all time,

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:32.760
but could, in fact, slowly change?

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.000
On his return, he sorted out his specimens

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.640
and sent them off to relevant experts

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.800
so that each could be identified and classified.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:54.520
Most of the mammal bones and fossils he sent to Richard Owen.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.040
Owen was one of the most brilliant zoologists of his time.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.520
He was the first to recognise dinosaurs

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.560
and, indeed, had invented their very name.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:08.040
And he would later become the creator and first director

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.560
of the Natural History Museum in London.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.520
But the question was,

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:13.440
was Darwin's

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:18.880
the creator and first director of the Natural History Museum in London?

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.600
Many of the specimens that Darwin collected

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.080
are still preserved and treasured here,

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.560
among the 70 million other specimens

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.640
housed in the museum that Owen founded.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:41.440
And here is one of them.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:44.360
It's obviously the lower jaw of some great animal.

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.120
And when Darwin discovered it,

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:49.040
it had bits of skin and hair attached to it,

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:50.680
so that at first it was thought

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:54.320
to be the remains of some unknown living species.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.960
But now we know that it is a species that was extinct

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:02.080
for some 10,000 years, a giant ground sloth.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:05.240
Owen examined it in great detail

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.560
and eventually described it

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.600
and gave it the name of Myelodon.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:13.360
The name of Myelodon darwinii,

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.160
in honour of its discoverer.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:20.400
But that mutual respect between two great men of science

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:22.120
was not to last.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:30.040
Soon after his return from his voyage,

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:34.120
Darwin made his home here in Down House in Kent.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.760
Here, he wrote an account of his travels

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:39.720
and worked on detailed scientific treatises

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.560
on corals and barnacles

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.400
and the geology and fossils of South America.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:49.320
But he also pondered deeply

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:53.160
on what he had seen in the Galapagos and elsewhere.

00:10:53.160 --> 00:11:16.840
Maybe species were not fixed.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:20.440
Every day, he took a walk in this small spinny

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:25.120
that he had planted at the end of his garden.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.760
And it was here that he came to ponder

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:29.800
on the problems of natural history,

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.680
including that mystery of mysteries,

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:36.280
how could one species turn into another?

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.520
He noted that most, if not all, animals

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.800
produce many more young than live to breed themselves.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:50.040
This female blue-tet, for example,

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.120
may well lay a dozen eggs a year,

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.280
perhaps 50 or so in her lifetime.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:58.880
Yet only two of her chicks need to survive and breed themselves

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:02.720
to maintain the numbers of the blue-tet population.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:04.440
Those survivors, of course,

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:06.880
are likely to be the healthiest and best suited

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:09.640
to their particular environment.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.920
Their characteristics are then inherited.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.200
So perhaps over many generations,

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:17.640
and particularly if there are environmental changes,

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:21.000
species may well change.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:23.800
Only the fittest survive.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.640
And that was the key.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:31.040
He called the process natural selection.

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:41.880
That would explain the differences that he had noted

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:46.560
in the finches that he had brought back from the Galapagos.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:50.120
They were very similar except for their beaks.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:53.360
This one has a very thin, delicate beak,

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.960
which it uses to catch insects.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:58.800
This one, on the other hand,

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:02.160
which came from an environment where there were a lot of nuts,

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:07.200
has a big, heavy beak, which enables it to crack them.

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:11.360
So maybe over the vastness of geological time,

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:15.920
and particularly if species were invading new environments,

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:20.880
those changes would amount to very radical changes indeed.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:34.840
Darwin drew a sketch in one of his notebooks

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.920
to illustrate his idea,

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.720
showing how a single ancestral species

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:44.200
might give rise to several different ones,

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:47.800
and then wrote above it a tentative...

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:49.880
I think.

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:00.880
Now he had to prove his theory,

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:06.120
and he spent years gathering abundant and convincing evidence.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:08.680
He was an extraordinary letter writer.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:11.680
He wrote as many as a dozen letters a day

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:14.600
to scientists and naturalists all over the world.

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:31.600
He also realised that when people had first started domesticating animals,

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.520
they had been doing experiments for him for centuries.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:40.520
All domestic dogs are descended from a single ancestral species,

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:41.920
the wolf.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.520
Dog breeders select those pups that have the characteristics

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.520
that happen to please them.

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:50.320
Nature, of course, selects those young animals

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.200
that are best suited to a particular environment.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.160
But the process is essentially the same,

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:00.720
and in both cases, it has produced astonishing variety.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:05.720
It seems good, McKay. Good boy. Good boy.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.800
In effect, many of these different breeds

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:13.440
could be considered different species

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:16.880
because they do not, indeed, they cannot interbreed

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:18.880
for purely mechanical reasons.

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:22.880
There's no way in which a Pekingese can mate with a Great Dane.

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:30.880
Of course, it's true that if you used artificial insemination,

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:34.400
you could get crosses between almost any of these breeds,

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:38.480
but that's because human beings have been selecting between dogs

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:40.560
for only a few centuries.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:45.640
Nature has been selecting between animals for millions of years.

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:48.720
And that's because they are different species.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:53.280
Nature has been selecting between animals for millions of years,

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:56.560
tens of millions, even hundreds of millions of years.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:00.720
So what might have started out as we would consider to be breeds

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:25.720
have now become so different they are species.

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:31.360
Darwin, sitting in Down House, wrote to pigeon fanciers and rabbit breeders

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:36.560
asking all kinds of detailed questions about their methods and results.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:40.360
He himself, being a country gentleman and running an estate,

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:43.960
knew about breeding horses and sheep and cattle.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:48.320
And he also conducted careful experiments with plants in his greenhouse.

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:52.320
But Darwin knew that the idea that species could appear

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:56.720
without divine intervention would appall society in general.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:00.320
And it was also contrary to the beliefs of his wife, Emma,

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:02.400
who was a devout Christian.

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:04.480
Perhaps for that reason,

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:08.560
he was keen to keep the focus of his work scientific.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:12.640
He made a point of not being drawn in public about his work.

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:16.720
He was keen to keep the focus of his work scientific.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:20.720
He was keen to keep the focus of his work scientific.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:25.560
But in the latter part of his life, he withdrew from attending church.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:30.000
On Sundays, he would escort Emma and the children here

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:32.080
to the parish church in Down,

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:34.800
but while they went into the service,

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:38.880
he remained outside and went for a walk in the country lanes.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.880
Perhaps because he feared that his theory would cause outrage

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:47.960
in some quarters, he delayed publishing it year after year after year.

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:51.040
But he wrote a long abstract of it.

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:57.120
And then on July 5th, 1844, he wrote this letter to his wife.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:03.200
My dear Emma, I have just finished this sketch of my species' life.

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:07.280
I have just finished this sketch of my species' life.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:10.280
I have just finished this sketch of my species' theory.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:14.360
Some sketch. It was 240 pages long.

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:18.440
I therefore write this in case of my sudden death

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:22.520
that you will devote £400 to its publication.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:26.600
He then goes on to list his various naturalist friends

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:29.680
who would be asked to edit it and check it.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.760
And he ends the letter, charmingly.

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:37.760
If yours affectionately, C.R. Darwin.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:50.640
He continued to accumulate evidence and refine his theory

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:53.720
for the next 14 years.

00:18:58.040 --> 00:19:01.040
But then his hand was forced.

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:07.040
In June 1858, 22 years after he got back from the Galapagos,

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:11.120
here in his study in Daan, he received a package

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:16.200
from a naturalist who was working in what is now Indonesia.

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:20.280
His name was Alfred Russell Wallace.

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:26.640
He had been corresponding with Darwin for some years.

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.720
But this package was different.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:34.480
It contained an essay that set out exactly the same idea as Darwin's,

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:37.560
of evolution by natural selection.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:43.160
The idea had come to Wallace as he lay in his hut,

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:46.840
semi-delirious in a malarial fever.

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:50.920
But although his idea of natural selection was the same as Darwin's,

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:56.000
he had not spent 20 years gathering the mountain of evidence to support it,

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:58.080
as Darwin had done.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:01.080
But whose idea was it?

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:04.760
In the end, the senior members of the Lunian Society

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:08.840
decided that the fairest thing was for a brief outline of the theory

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:11.920
from each of them to be read out one after the other

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.000
at a meeting of the Society here in Burlington House in London.

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:21.080
The Lunian, then, as now, was the place where scientists

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:24.160
studying the natural world held regular meetings

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:29.160
to present and discuss papers about their observations and thoughts.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:34.640
The one held on July 1, 1858,

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:38.720
was attended by only about 30 people.

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:41.360
Neither of the authors were present.

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:45.160
Wallace was 10,000 miles away in the East Indies,

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:50.040
and Darwin was ill and devastated by the death a few days earlier

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:52.120
of his infant son.

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:55.120
So he was still at his home in Kent.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:21:00.200
As a consequence, the two papers had to be read by the secretary.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:05.280
And as far as we can tell, they made very little impression on anyone.

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:11.920
Darwin spent the next year writing out his theory in detail.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:16.000
Then he sent the manuscript to his publisher, John Murray,

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:20.080
whose firm then as now had offices in Albemarle Street,

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:22.080
just off Piccadilly in London.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:27.000
Murray was the great publisher of his day

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:30.080
and dealt with the works of Jane Austen and Lord Barron,

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:34.160
whose first editions still lie in these office walls.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:38.240
Darwin regarded his work as simply a summary,

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:41.320
but even so, it's 400 pages.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:46.400
It was published on November 24, 1859.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:49.400
This is not a first edition, more so a pity.

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:53.480
First editions are worth literally hundreds of thousands of pounds.

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:58.560
This is a sixth edition, my copy, which I bought as a boy.

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:00.640
I was 18, I noticed,

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:04.720
and it cost me the princely sum of one shilling.

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:14.480
The first edition of 1,250 copies sold out immediately,

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:18.480
and it went for a reprint and then another reprint and another reprint.

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:21.560
It's a book that contains very few technical terms.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:24.640
It's easily understood by anybody.

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:27.720
And predictably, it caused an outrage,

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:31.800
not only throughout this country, but indeed all the civilised world.

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:38.600
What scandalised people most, it seems,

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:42.680
was the implication that human beings were not specially created

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.680
by God, as the Book of Genesis stated,

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:48.760
but were descended from ape-like ancestors,

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:52.840
a notion that provided a lot of scope for cartoonists.

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:58.080
The leaders of the church, headed by Samuel Wilberforce,

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:02.160
the Bishop of Oxford, attacked it on the grounds that it demoted God

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:06.240
and contradicted the story of creation as told by the Bible.

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:10.240
That Mr Darwin should have wandered from this broad highway

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:14.320
of nature's works into the jungle of fanciful assumption

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:16.400
is no small evil.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:19.480
I have read your book with more pain than pleasure.

00:23:19.560 --> 00:23:23.560
It is the frenzied inspiration of the inhaler of mephitic gas.

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:25.640
Fails utterly.

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:33.480
Darwin's theory implied that life had a purpose.

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:38.480
Darwin's theory implied that life had originated in simple forms

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:41.560
and had then become more and more complex.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:45.640
He knew perfectly well that the whole idea of evolution

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:47.720
raised a lot of questions.

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:52.080
In fact, some of those questions would not be answered

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:56.160
until comparatively recently, but in his own time,

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:59.240
many distinguished scientists raised what seemed to be

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:01.320
insuperable difficulties.

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:05.320
And foremost among them was Richard Owen,

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:09.400
the man who 20 years earlier had named the extinct ground sloth

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:11.480
in honour of Darwin.

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:17.640
Over the years, the two men had developed a deep personal dislike

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:21.720
of one another and had quarrelled frequently.

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:26.800
It wasn't that Owen thought that the story of the Garden of Eden

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:28.880
was literally correct,

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:31.880
but nonetheless he was a deeply religious man.

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:41.240
He had, after all, ensured that his museum,

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:44.320
which would display the wonders of creation,

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:48.400
echoed in its design the great Christian cathedrals

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:59.400
of medieval Europe.

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:03.480
And Owen knew about the diversity of life.

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:07.560
Indeed, he had spent his whole career cataloguing it.

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:10.640
But even so, he refused to believe that a species

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:12.720
could change over time.

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:18.200
He and other pioneer Victorian geologists

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:21.200
as they established their comparatively new science,

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:24.280
recognised that the outlines of the history of life

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:28.360
could be deduced by examining the land around them.

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:35.080
Look at these rocks in northern Scotland.

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:38.160
We know from fossils that are associated with them

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:40.240
that they are very ancient.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:43.320
And they are sandstones,

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:47.400
compacted sand that was laid down at the bottom of the sea,

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:50.400
layer upon layer upon layer.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:53.480
But look how many layers there are.

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:12.200
Clearly, those at the top must have been laid down

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:14.280
after those beneath them.

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:17.280
So, as you descend from layer to layer,

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:20.360
you are, in effect, going back in time.

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:25.760
So, a fossil species, if it comes from a particular layer,

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:28.840
is of a particular age.

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:30.920
And if you can recognise each one,

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.000
then you can begin to piece together

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:35.080
the outlines of life's history.

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:40.160
My craster.

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:43.240
The ability to identify fossils

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:45.240
and place them in their geological time zone

00:26:45.320 --> 00:26:47.320
was still an essential skill

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:50.400
when I was at university a century later.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:54.480
We worked our way through drawers like these,

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:58.560
which are full of fossils of one sort or another,

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:01.640
but none of them have labels, only numbers.

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:05.720
So, you were expected to be able to pick up one

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:10.800
and say, yes, that's a bellum knight.

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:14.800
Now, actually, which bellum knight it is, I can't remember now.

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:17.880
When you came to a practical exam,

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:20.960
your examiners would produce one of these and say,

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:23.040
OK, what's that?

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:25.120
And you either knew or you didn't.

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:28.200
And the way you knew, because of all the work you did

00:27:28.280 --> 00:27:31.280
in drawers like these, hour after hour.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:38.080
Owen did not deny the sequence

00:27:38.080 --> 00:27:41.080
in which all these different species appeared,

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:43.160
but he believed that each was separate,

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:45.240
each divinely created.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:47.320
Darwin's theory, however,

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:49.400
required that there should be connections,

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:51.480
not just between similar species,

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:54.560
but between the great animal groups.

00:27:57.600 --> 00:28:01.600
If fishes and reptiles and birds and mammals

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:03.680
had all evolved from one another,

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:06.760
then surely there must be intermediate forms

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:08.760
between those great groups.

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:10.840
And they were missing.

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:14.920
And then, just two years after the publication

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.000
of the Origin of Species,

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:19.080
Richard Owen himself

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:23.160
purchased the most astonishing fossil for his museum.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:30.200
It had been found in this limestone quarry in Bavaria.

00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:34.280
The stone here splits into flat, smooth leaves

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:38.280
that have been used as roofing tiles since Roman times.

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:42.120
Most are blank.

00:28:42.200 --> 00:28:45.200
But occasionally, when you split them apart,

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:48.280
they reveal a shrimp or a fish.

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:52.640
It's almost impossible to resist the temptation

00:28:52.720 --> 00:28:55.720
of pulling down almost every boulder you see

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.800
and then opening it like a book

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:00.880
to look at each unopened page

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:05.880
to see whether maybe it contains yet another fossil.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:15.880
But this fossil was something unprecedented.

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:18.960
It is still one of the greatest of the treasures

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:22.040
that are stored in the Natural History Museum.

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:24.120
And this is it.

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:27.200
It's called Archaeopteryx.

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:30.280
It has unmistakable features.

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:34.280
It has unmistakable feathers on its wings

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:36.360
and down its tail.

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:40.440
So Owen had no hesitation in calling it a bird.

00:29:40.520 --> 00:29:44.520
But it was unlike any other bird that anyone knew of

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:48.600
because it had claws on the front of its wings.

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.680
And, as was later discovered,

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:53.760
it didn't have a beak but jaws with teeth in it

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:57.840
and a line of bones supporting its tail.

00:29:57.840 --> 00:30:01.840
So it was part reptile, part bird.

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:06.760
Here was a link between those two great groups

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:08.840
that was no longer missing.

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:13.920
Gosh, you really can see the filaments now.

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:21.000
Other examples of the same creature

00:30:21.080 --> 00:30:24.080
show its feathers even more clearly.

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:27.080
We know from the bones of Archaeopteryx

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:31.160
that it was, at best, a very poor flyer.

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:34.240
So it's not surprising that, eventually,

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:38.320
it was superseded by more modern, more efficient birds.

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:42.400
And that's the fate of these links between great groups.

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:45.480
Eventually, they become extinct.

00:30:45.560 --> 00:30:48.560
And the only way we know they existed

00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:50.640
is from their fossilised remains.

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:54.640
Even so, there is a bird alive today

00:30:54.720 --> 00:31:00.720
that illustrates the link between modern birds and reptiles.

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:07.360
The hoatzin nests in the swamps of tropical South America.

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:09.440
There are caiman in the water beneath,

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:12.520
ready to snap up any chick that might fall from its nest,

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:15.600
so an ability to hold on tight is very valuable.

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:18.680
And the nestling is a very valuable resource

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:20.680
and it's very valuable.

00:31:20.760 --> 00:31:24.760
And the nestlings have a very interesting way of doing that.

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:27.840
The young still have claws on the front of their wings,

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:29.920
as Archaeopteryx did.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:33.000
Here is vivid evidence that the wings of birds

00:31:33.080 --> 00:31:37.080
are modified forelegs and ones had toes with claws on them.

00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:41.080
There's another creature alive today

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:44.160
that represents the link between the great animal groups,

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.240
a descendant of a group of reptiles

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:49.240
that took a different evolutionary course

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:52.320
and evolved not feathers but fur.

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:54.400
The platypus.

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:58.480
When specimens of this creature first reached Europe from Australia

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:00.560
at the very end of the 18th century,

00:32:00.640 --> 00:32:03.640
people refused to believe their eyes.

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:06.720
They said it was a hoax,

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:09.800
bits and pieces of different creatures

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:12.880
rather crudely sewn together.

00:32:12.960 --> 00:32:15.960
And yet, in a way, those early sceptics were right.

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:18.960
The platypus is the most extraordinary mixture

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:21.040
of different animals.

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:23.120
It's part mammal and part reptile.

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:25.200
And so it can give us some idea

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:27.280
of how the first mammals developed.

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:31.080
When it comes to breed,

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:35.160
it does something that separates it from all other mammals except one.

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:39.240
In its nest, deep in a burrow, it lays eggs.

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:42.320
It's this that links the platypus with the reptiles,

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:47.320
and this that entitles it to be regarded as the most primitive living mammal.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:53.320
So, the links between the great animal groups

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:55.400
are not, in fact, missing,

00:32:55.480 --> 00:32:58.480
but exist both as fossils and as living animals.

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:06.440
Although the fossil record provides an answer to the problem of missing links,

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:10.520
it also posed a major problem.

00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:13.520
It started very abruptly.

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:16.600
The earliest known fossils in Darwin's time

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:19.680
came from a formation called the Cambrian.

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:22.760
And there were two main kinds.

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:26.840
These, which look like phretzor blades and are called graptolite,

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:31.920
and these, like giant woodlice, which are called trilobites.

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:36.000
Could it really be that life on Earth started

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:41.000
with creatures as complex as these?

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:59.240
As a boy, I was a passionate collector of fossils.

00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:02.320
I grew up in the city of Lestane,

00:34:02.320 --> 00:34:07.320
and I knew that in this area, not far from the city, called Childwood Forest,

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:10.400
there were the oldest rocks in the world,

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:12.480
older even than the Cambrian.

00:34:12.560 --> 00:34:17.560
So, therefore, by definition, they would be without fossils.

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:32.560
There was no point in me looking for fossils in these ancient rocks.

00:34:38.240 --> 00:34:41.240
There were, it's true, very rarely,

00:34:41.320 --> 00:34:46.320
some rather odd shapes in these rocks, like this one here.

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:51.320
But they were dismissed as being some kind of mechanical aberration.

00:34:51.400 --> 00:34:55.400
I mean, after all, how could there be anything living

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:58.480
in these extremely ancient rocks?

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:03.560
And then, in 1957, their schoolboy,

00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:08.640
with rather more patience and perspicacity than I had,

00:35:08.720 --> 00:35:11.720
found something really remarkable,

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:16.720
and undeniably the remains of a living creature.

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:24.240
And here it is in Leicester Museum, where it's been brought for safekeeping.

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:26.320
It's called Charnia.

00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:31.400
Who could doubt that this is the impression of a living organism?

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:37.480
It has a central stem, branches on either side.

00:35:37.480 --> 00:35:42.480
In fact, it seems to have been something like the sea pens

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:45.560
that today grow on coral reefs.

00:35:45.640 --> 00:35:50.640
Since its discovery, a whole range of organisms have been found

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:53.720
in rocks of this extreme age,

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:56.800
not only here in the Charnwood Forest,

00:35:56.880 --> 00:35:59.880
but in many other different parts of the world.

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:03.960
Fossil hunters searching these rocks

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:06.040
in the Ediacra Hills of Australia

00:36:06.040 --> 00:36:09.040
had also been discovering other strange shapes.

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:14.320
At first, many scientists refused to believe

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:18.400
that these faint impressions were the remains of jellyfish.

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:21.480
But by now, enough specimens have been discovered

00:36:21.560 --> 00:36:24.560
to make quite sure that that indeed is what they are.

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:28.560
So now we know that life did not begin suddenly

00:36:28.640 --> 00:36:31.640
with those complex animals of the Cambrian.

00:36:32.920 --> 00:36:34.920
It started much, much earlier,

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:40.000
first with simple microscopic forms, which eventually became bigger,

00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:42.600
but which were still so soft and delicate

00:36:42.680 --> 00:36:45.680
that they only very rarely left any marks.

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:49.180
But the

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:55.180
The question of the age of the Earth posed another problem for Darwin's theory.

00:36:55.260 --> 00:37:00.260
In the 17th century, an Irish bishop had used the genealogies

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:03.340
recorded in the Bible that lead back to Adam

00:37:03.420 --> 00:37:06.420
to work out that the week of creation must have taken place

00:37:06.500 --> 00:37:09.500
in the year 4004 BC.

00:37:09.580 --> 00:37:13.140
The first

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:16.220
and last century, the first century,

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:19.220
was in the year 4004 BC.

00:37:19.300 --> 00:37:23.300
That may seem to us to be a very naive way of doing things,

00:37:23.380 --> 00:37:25.380
but what other method was there anyway?

00:37:27.100 --> 00:37:30.100
The Victorian geologists had already concluded

00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:33.180
that the Earth must be millions of years old,

00:37:33.260 --> 00:37:36.260
but how many millions, no-one could say.

00:37:37.460 --> 00:37:41.460
Then, less than 50 years after the publication of the origin,

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:45.540
a discovery was made in what seemed a totally disconnected branch

00:37:45.540 --> 00:37:48.540
of science that would ultimately provide the answer.

00:37:50.460 --> 00:37:53.460
A Polish woman working in Paris, Marie Curie,

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:57.540
discovered that some rocks contained an element called uranium

00:37:57.620 --> 00:38:00.620
that decays over time at a steady rate

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:02.700
through a process called radiation.

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:08.780
Today, a century after she made her extraordinary discovery,

00:38:08.860 --> 00:38:12.860
the method of dating by measuring changes in radioactivity

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:15.860
has become greatly refined.

00:38:18.780 --> 00:38:21.780
This is a sample taken from those very ancient rocks

00:38:21.860 --> 00:38:23.860
in Charlemont Forest,

00:38:23.940 --> 00:38:31.940
and these tiny crystals are revealed to be 562 million years old.

00:38:32.020 --> 00:38:36.020
That provides more than enough time for natural selection

00:38:36.100 --> 00:38:38.100
to produce the procession of fossils

00:38:38.180 --> 00:38:41.180
that eventually leads to the living animals and plants

00:38:41.180 --> 00:38:43.180
we know today.

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:46.780
But there was another objection.

00:38:46.860 --> 00:38:50.860
If all animals within a group have a common origin,

00:38:50.940 --> 00:38:53.940
how is it that some kinds of animals

00:38:54.020 --> 00:38:57.020
are distributed throughout the continents of the world,

00:38:57.100 --> 00:38:59.100
except for Antarctica?

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:04.180
How is it that, for example, frogs in Europe and Africa

00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:07.260
are also found here in South America,

00:39:07.340 --> 00:39:09.340
on the other side of the Atlantic Ocean,

00:39:09.340 --> 00:39:13.340
bearing in mind that frogs have permeable skins

00:39:13.420 --> 00:39:16.420
and can't survive in seawater?

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:20.500
Darwin himself had a couple of suggestions.

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:23.580
One was that they might have floated across accidentally

00:39:23.660 --> 00:39:25.660
on rafts of vegetation,

00:39:25.740 --> 00:39:28.740
and the other is that maybe there were land bridges

00:39:28.820 --> 00:39:30.820
between the continents.

00:39:30.820 --> 00:39:40.820
He was not convinced by either explanation.

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:43.900
Even as late as 1947,

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:46.980
when I was a geology student here at Cambridge,

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:50.060
there was no convincing explanation.

00:39:50.140 --> 00:39:53.140
It's true that back in 1912,

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:56.220
a German geologist had suggested that,

00:39:56.300 --> 00:40:00.300
at one time in the very remote distant past,

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:03.300
all the continents of the Earth that we know today

00:40:03.380 --> 00:40:07.380
were grouped together to form one huge supercontinent,

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:13.460
and that, over time, this broke up and the pieces drifted apart.

00:40:13.540 --> 00:40:17.540
That would have provided an answer.

00:40:17.620 --> 00:40:20.620
But when I asked the professor of geology here,

00:40:20.700 --> 00:40:23.700
who was lecturing to us, why he didn't tell us about that

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:28.780
in his lectures, he replied rather lofty, I must say,

00:40:28.780 --> 00:40:32.780
when you can demonstrate to me that there is a force on Earth

00:40:32.860 --> 00:40:35.860
that can move the continents by a millimetre,

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:37.940
I will consider it.

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:42.020
But until then, the idea is sheer moonshine, dear boy.

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:47.180
But then, in the 1960s,

00:40:47.260 --> 00:40:51.260
it became possible to map the sea floor in detail.

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:55.340
And it was discovered not only that the continents have shifted

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:59.340
in just the way that the German geologist had suggested,

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:01.420
but that they were still moving.

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:06.540
New rock wells up from deep below the Earth's crust

00:41:06.620 --> 00:41:10.020
and flows away on either side of the mid-ocean ridges,

00:41:10.100 --> 00:41:12.100
carrying the continents with it.

00:41:13.580 --> 00:41:17.500
Amphibians had originally evolved on this supercontinent

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:20.500
and had then travelled on each of its various fragments

00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:22.740
as they drifted apart.

00:41:22.740 --> 00:41:25.740
Problem solved.

00:41:28.180 --> 00:41:32.020
Perhaps the biggest problem of all for most people

00:41:32.100 --> 00:41:36.780
was the argument put forward for the existence of God

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:38.860
at the beginning of the 19th century

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:42.580
by an Anglican clergyman called William Paley.

00:41:42.660 --> 00:41:46.020
He said, supposing you were walking in the countryside

00:41:46.100 --> 00:41:49.740
and you picked up something like this,

00:41:49.740 --> 00:41:52.820
you would know from looking at it

00:41:52.900 --> 00:41:55.900
that it had been designed to tell the time.

00:41:59.460 --> 00:42:02.460
There must, therefore, be a designer.

00:42:02.540 --> 00:42:04.540
And the same argument would apply

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:08.060
if you looked at one of the intricate structures found in nature,

00:42:08.140 --> 00:42:10.300
such as the human eye.

00:42:10.380 --> 00:42:14.380
And the only designer of the human eye could be God.

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:18.100
Anti-evolutionists maintain

00:42:18.100 --> 00:42:24.100
that the eye would only work if it was complete in all its details.

00:42:24.180 --> 00:42:28.100
Darwin, on the other hand, argued that the eye had developed,

00:42:28.180 --> 00:42:33.380
becoming increasingly complex over a long period of time.

00:42:33.460 --> 00:42:36.940
That would only work if each stage of development

00:42:37.020 --> 00:42:39.700
was an improvement on the previous one.

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:43.340
And today we know enough about the animal kingdom

00:42:43.420 --> 00:42:46.420
to know that that is indeed the case.

00:42:46.420 --> 00:42:51.100
Some very simple animals have nothing more than light-sensitive spots

00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:54.980
that enable them to tell the difference between light and dark.

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:59.940
But if a patch of such spots formed even the shallowest of pits,

00:43:00.020 --> 00:43:02.420
one edge of the pit would throw a shadow

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:05.260
and so reveal the direction of light.

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:09.420
If the pit got deeper and started to close,

00:43:09.500 --> 00:43:12.420
then light would form a blurred image.

00:43:12.420 --> 00:43:16.780
Mucus secreted by the cells would bend the light and focus it.

00:43:16.860 --> 00:43:20.580
If this mucus hardened, it would form a proper lens

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:23.580
and transmit a brighter and clearer image.

00:43:24.940 --> 00:43:27.980
All these different fully functional stages

00:43:28.060 --> 00:43:30.300
at different levels of complexity

00:43:30.380 --> 00:43:32.980
are found in living animals today.

00:43:33.060 --> 00:43:35.500
This single-celled creature

00:43:35.580 --> 00:43:38.300
has one of those light-sensitive spots.

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:42.220
Flatworms have a small pit containing light spots

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:45.300
so they can detect the shadow of a predator.

00:43:46.220 --> 00:43:48.580
A snail's blurry vision is good enough

00:43:48.660 --> 00:43:51.020
to enable it to find its way to food.

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:55.020
And the octopus has an eye with a proper lens

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:57.460
and can see as much detail as we can.

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:05.580
So the structure of the human eye

00:44:05.580 --> 00:44:08.740
does not demand the assistance of a supernatural designer.

00:44:08.820 --> 00:44:11.820
It can have evolved gradually with each stage

00:44:11.900 --> 00:44:15.740
bringing a real advantage, as Darwin's theory demands.

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:23.460
Natural selection, of course,

00:44:23.540 --> 00:44:26.060
requires that an animal's characteristics

00:44:26.140 --> 00:44:28.780
are handed from one generation to the next.

00:44:28.860 --> 00:44:32.580
It's obvious that children resemble their parents.

00:44:32.580 --> 00:44:36.060
Anyone knows that. But when you come to think of it,

00:44:36.140 --> 00:44:38.140
how does that come about?

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:43.140
In Darwin's time, nobody had the faintest idea

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:47.740
about the mechanism or the rules that governed that process.

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:50.420
Except perhaps for one man

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:53.220
who was working in the city of Brno,

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:55.500
in what is now the Czech Republic,

00:44:55.580 --> 00:44:58.740
at exactly the same time that Darwin is writing his book

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:02.700
in Kent. That man's name was Gregor Mendel.

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:07.300
He discovered the laws of inheritance

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:09.900
by breeding thousands of pea plants

00:45:09.980 --> 00:45:13.780
and observing how they changed from one generation to the next.

00:45:15.260 --> 00:45:17.820
He found that while many characteristics

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:21.300
were passed down directly from one generation to another,

00:45:21.380 --> 00:45:24.020
others could actually skip a generation.

00:45:24.100 --> 00:45:25.780
How could that happen?

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:29.940
Mendel explained this by suggesting that each plant,

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:33.780
each organism, contained within it factors

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:36.700
which were responsible for creating

00:45:36.780 --> 00:45:39.300
those particular characteristics.

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:42.820
Today, we call those things genes.

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:45.820
But nobody had any idea how they worked

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:49.500
until 100 years after Mendel's time.

00:45:49.580 --> 00:45:51.500
And then the answer was,

00:45:51.500 --> 00:45:56.180
in Cambridge.

00:45:56.260 --> 00:46:00.260
In 1953, here in the Cavendish Laboratories,

00:46:00.340 --> 00:46:01.980
two young researchers,

00:46:02.060 --> 00:46:04.380
Francis Crick and James Watson,

00:46:04.460 --> 00:46:07.500
were building models like this.

00:46:07.580 --> 00:46:11.500
It was their way of thinking about and investigating

00:46:11.580 --> 00:46:14.540
the structure of a complex molecule

00:46:14.620 --> 00:46:17.220
that's found in the human body.

00:46:17.300 --> 00:46:19.500
And they were able to do that

00:46:19.500 --> 00:46:23.460
with a complex molecule that's found in the genes of all animals,

00:46:23.540 --> 00:46:25.340
DNA.

00:46:26.740 --> 00:46:29.980
The crucial bit are these chains

00:46:31.220 --> 00:46:32.620
which encircle the rod.

00:46:35.500 --> 00:46:38.460
And here is a second, and entwined.

00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:42.340
This is the double helix.

00:46:44.860 --> 00:46:47.260
The workings of the DNA molecule

00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:49.500
are now understood in such detail

00:46:49.580 --> 00:46:53.100
that we can demonstrate something that is truly astounding.

00:46:54.380 --> 00:46:58.300
A gene taken from one animal can function in another.

00:46:59.660 --> 00:47:03.420
The gene that causes the jellyfish to be luminous, for example,

00:47:03.500 --> 00:47:05.900
transplanted into a mouse,

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:08.020
will make that mouse luminous.

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:11.860
The genetic code can also reveal relationships.

00:47:11.940 --> 00:47:15.940
Even our law courts accept that DNA fingerprinting

00:47:16.020 --> 00:47:19.860
can establish whether a man is the father of a particular child.

00:47:21.700 --> 00:47:23.460
And it can also reveal

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:27.100
whether one kind of animal is related to another.

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:33.300
The genetic code is a very important part of the DNA code.

00:47:33.380 --> 00:47:35.980
It's a very important part of the DNA code.

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:38.300
It's a very important part of the DNA code.

00:47:43.340 --> 00:47:46.500
It proves, for example, that kangaroos,

00:47:46.580 --> 00:47:49.660
ground-living animals that run with great leaps,

00:47:49.740 --> 00:47:55.060
are closely related to koalas that have taken to climbing trees.

00:47:56.260 --> 00:47:59.860
That insect-eating shrews have cousins

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:03.340
that took to the air in search of insects, bats.

00:48:03.340 --> 00:48:06.500
The elephant family, way back in geological history,

00:48:06.580 --> 00:48:09.820
took to the water and became sea cows.

00:48:11.500 --> 00:48:17.980
So, 150 years after the publication of Darwin's revolutionary book,

00:48:18.060 --> 00:48:22.620
modern genetics has confirmed its fundamental truth.

00:48:22.700 --> 00:48:25.660
All life is related.

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:29.220
And it enables us to construct with confidence

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:33.220
the complex tree that represents the history

00:48:33.220 --> 00:48:35.220
of life.

00:48:35.300 --> 00:48:39.980
It began in the sea some 3,000 million years ago.

00:48:41.140 --> 00:48:45.860
Complex chemical molecules began to clump together

00:48:45.940 --> 00:48:49.260
to form microscopic blobs, cells.

00:48:51.020 --> 00:48:55.020
These were the seeds from which the tree of life developed.

00:48:55.100 --> 00:48:59.980
They were able to split, replicating themselves as bacteria do.

00:48:59.980 --> 00:49:03.980
And as time passed, they diversified into different groups.

00:49:05.540 --> 00:49:09.460
Some remained attached to one another so that they formed chains.

00:49:09.540 --> 00:49:11.540
We know them today as algae.

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:17.020
Others formed hollow balls which collapsed upon themselves,

00:49:17.100 --> 00:49:19.660
creating a body with an internal cavity.

00:49:20.940 --> 00:49:24.140
They were the first multi-celled organisms.

00:49:24.220 --> 00:49:26.980
Sponges are their direct descendants.

00:49:26.980 --> 00:49:30.700
As more variations appeared, the tree of life grew

00:49:30.780 --> 00:49:32.780
and became more diverse.

00:49:32.860 --> 00:49:35.380
Some organisms became more mobile

00:49:35.460 --> 00:49:38.500
and developed a mouth that opened into a gut.

00:49:41.820 --> 00:49:45.660
Others had bodies stiffened by an internal rod.

00:49:46.620 --> 00:49:50.660
They, understandably, developed sense organs around their front end.

00:49:50.660 --> 00:49:55.180
A related group had bodies that were divided into segments,

00:49:55.260 --> 00:49:57.260
with little projections on either side

00:49:57.340 --> 00:49:59.940
that helped them to move around on the sea floor.

00:50:00.020 --> 00:50:04.180
Some of these segmented creatures developed hard protective skins

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:06.260
which gave their bodies some rigidity.

00:50:07.340 --> 00:50:11.820
So now the seas were filled with a great variety of animals.

00:50:13.420 --> 00:50:16.740
And then, around 450 million years ago,

00:50:16.820 --> 00:50:19.700
some of the most important species of life

00:50:19.700 --> 00:50:22.260
were killed, some of these armoured creatures

00:50:22.340 --> 00:50:25.940
crawled up out of the water and ventured onto land.

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:33.540
And here, the tree of life branched into a multitude of different species

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:37.300
that exploited this new environment in all kinds of ways.

00:50:39.700 --> 00:50:43.460
One group of them developed elongated flaps on their backs,

00:50:43.540 --> 00:50:47.260
which over many generations eventually developed into wings.

00:50:47.260 --> 00:50:49.660
The insects had arrived.

00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:55.740
Life moved into the air and diversified into myriad forms.

00:50:58.420 --> 00:51:00.540
Meanwhile, back in the seas,

00:51:00.620 --> 00:51:03.260
those creatures with a stiffening rod in their bodies

00:51:03.340 --> 00:51:06.180
had strengthened it by encasing it in bone.

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:11.140
A skull developed with a hinged jaw

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:13.220
that could grab and hold onto prey.

00:51:13.220 --> 00:51:16.860
They grew bigger and developed fins equipped with muscles

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:19.900
that enabled them to swim with speed and power.

00:51:21.300 --> 00:51:25.140
So fish now dominated the waters of the world.

00:51:26.020 --> 00:51:29.300
One group of them developed the ability to gulp air

00:51:29.380 --> 00:51:31.380
from the water surface.

00:51:33.860 --> 00:51:37.260
Their fleshy fins became weight-supporting lakes

00:51:37.340 --> 00:51:42.020
and 375 million years ago, a few of them were killed

00:51:42.020 --> 00:51:44.860
and a few of these backbone creatures

00:51:44.940 --> 00:51:47.500
followed the insects onto the land.

00:51:48.900 --> 00:51:51.380
They were amphibians with wet skins

00:51:51.460 --> 00:51:54.740
and they had to return to water to lay their eggs.

00:51:54.820 --> 00:51:59.220
But some of their descendants evolved dry, scaly skins

00:51:59.300 --> 00:52:03.780
and broke their link with water by laying eggs with watertight shells.

00:52:05.460 --> 00:52:07.300
These creatures, the reptiles,

00:52:07.300 --> 00:52:12.060
were the ancestors of today's tortoises, snakes, lizards and crocodiles.

00:52:13.140 --> 00:52:16.660
And, of course, they included the group that, back then,

00:52:16.740 --> 00:52:19.940
came to dominate the land, the dinosaurs.

00:52:22.900 --> 00:52:27.100
But 65 million years ago, a great disaster overtook the Earth.

00:52:32.420 --> 00:52:36.940
Whatever its cause, a great proportion of animals were exterminated.

00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:40.580
All the dinosaurs disappeared except for one branch

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:43.380
whose scales had become modified into feathers.

00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:47.060
They were the birds.

00:52:47.140 --> 00:52:49.020
While they spread through the skies,

00:52:49.100 --> 00:52:52.740
a small, seemingly insignificant group of survivors

00:52:52.820 --> 00:52:55.620
began to increase in numbers on the ground beneath.

00:52:57.180 --> 00:53:00.100
These creatures differed from their competitors

00:53:00.180 --> 00:53:04.580
in that their bodies were warm and insulated with coats of fur.

00:53:04.580 --> 00:53:07.100
They were the first mammals.

00:53:07.180 --> 00:53:10.500
With much of the land left vacant after the great catastrophe,

00:53:10.580 --> 00:53:13.420
they now had their chance.

00:53:13.500 --> 00:53:15.380
Their warm, insulated bodies

00:53:15.460 --> 00:53:20.940
enabled them to be active at all times, at night as well as during the day,

00:53:21.020 --> 00:53:24.900
and in all places, from the Arctic to the tropics,

00:53:26.060 --> 00:53:29.700
in water as well as on land,

00:53:29.700 --> 00:53:34.700
on grassy plains and up in the trees.

00:54:00.700 --> 00:54:03.700
THEY COUGH

00:54:13.780 --> 00:54:19.980
There can be no doubt about our close relationship to these chimpanzees.

00:54:20.060 --> 00:54:22.300
Our bodies are so similar.

00:54:22.380 --> 00:54:26.100
The proportions of our limbs or our faces may differ,

00:54:26.180 --> 00:54:29.220
but otherwise we are very, very similar.

00:54:29.220 --> 00:54:32.300
The arrangement of our internal organs,

00:54:32.380 --> 00:54:36.580
the chemistry of our blood, the way our bodies work,

00:54:36.660 --> 00:54:39.260
all these are almost identical,

00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:42.500
and DNA confirms that.

00:54:42.580 --> 00:54:47.420
Indeed, we are as closely related to chimpanzees

00:54:47.500 --> 00:54:52.300
and the rest of the apes and monkeys as, say, lions are to tigers

00:54:52.300 --> 00:54:59.300
and to the rest of the cat family.

00:54:59.300 --> 00:55:04.300
MUSIC PLAYS

00:55:18.740 --> 00:55:24.220
Suddenly, an image from our remote past comes vivid little light.

00:55:24.300 --> 00:55:26.580
The time when our distant ancestors,

00:55:26.580 --> 00:55:29.260
in order to keep up with the changing environment,

00:55:29.340 --> 00:55:34.820
had to wade and keep their heads above water in order to find food.

00:55:34.900 --> 00:55:38.780
That crucial moment when our far distant ancestors

00:55:38.780 --> 00:55:59.780
took a step away from being apes and a step towards humanity.

00:55:59.860 --> 00:56:01.500
The Natural History Museum

00:56:01.580 --> 00:56:05.460
is one of the most important museums of its kind in the world.

00:56:05.540 --> 00:56:08.220
Richard Owen brought it into existence,

00:56:08.220 --> 00:56:10.060
but over a century later,

00:56:10.140 --> 00:56:12.700
discoveries from many branches of science

00:56:12.780 --> 00:56:16.220
have shown that his belief that species can never change

00:56:16.220 --> 00:56:40.140
but always remain exactly the same was mistaken.

00:56:40.220 --> 00:56:43.740
It was Charles Darwin's profound insights

00:56:43.740 --> 00:56:46.220
that have proved to be true.

00:56:46.300 --> 00:56:50.660
And now, to mark the 200th anniversary of his birth,

00:56:50.740 --> 00:56:54.700
his statue is being taken from its out-of-the-way location

00:56:54.700 --> 00:57:14.380
to be placed centre stage in the main hall.

00:57:14.460 --> 00:57:19.580
Darwin's great insight revolutionised the way in which we see the world.

00:57:19.660 --> 00:57:23.180
We now understand why there are so many different species,

00:57:23.180 --> 00:57:27.340
why they are distributed in the way they are around the world,

00:57:27.420 --> 00:57:32.580
and why their bodies and our bodies are shaped in the way that they are.

00:57:32.660 --> 00:57:35.940
Because we understand that bacteria evolve,

00:57:36.020 --> 00:57:40.020
we can devise methods of dealing with the diseases they cause.

00:57:40.100 --> 00:57:43.460
And because we can disentangle the complex relationships

00:57:43.540 --> 00:57:46.740
between animals and plants in a natural community,

00:57:46.820 --> 00:57:48.740
we can foresee some of the consequences

00:57:48.740 --> 00:57:53.740
when we start to interfere with those communities.

00:57:53.820 --> 00:57:56.660
But above all, Darwin has shown us

00:57:56.740 --> 00:58:00.260
that we are not apart from the natural world.

00:58:00.340 --> 00:58:03.780
We do not have dominion over it.

00:58:03.860 --> 00:58:07.660
We are subject to its laws and processes,

00:58:07.740 --> 00:58:10.940
as are all other animals on Earth,

00:58:10.940 --> 00:58:18.940
to which indeed we are related.
